# PART 5 — UNIVERSAL PRACTICAL SKILLS

## Variables

Independent variable:
Factor deliberately changed.

Dependent variable:
Factor measured.

Control variables:
Factors maintained constant.

Control treatment:
A reference condition used to interpret experimental results.

## Reliability

Reliability concerns consistency of measurements.

Improvements:
- Repeat measurements.
- Use larger appropriate samples.
- Standardise procedures.
- Reduce random errors.

## Validity

Validity concerns whether the method measures what
it is intended to measure.

Improvements:
- Control confounding variables.
- Use suitable control treatments.
- Measure the correct dependent variable.
- Avoid systematic bias.

## Accuracy

Accuracy concerns closeness to the true value.

Improvements:
- Calibrate instruments.
- Use appropriate measuring equipment.
- Correct systematic measurement errors.

## Precision

Precision concerns closeness of repeated measurements.

Higher-resolution instruments may improve precision
but do not automatically improve accuracy.

## Uncertainty

Percentage uncertainty =
absolute uncertainty / measured value × 100

## Percentage Change

Percentage change =
(final value - initial value) / initial value × 100

## Rate

Rate = change in measured quantity / time

## Percentage Cover

Percentage cover =
number of occupied sampling points /
total sampling points × 100

